{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"group-fastwr830","type":"group","x":2240,"y":1600,"width":540,"height":2980,"label":"Fast Wrapper Build"},
		{"id":"group-fastwr21f","type":"group","x":2240,"y":4720,"width":540,"height":1560,"label":"Fast Wrapper Validation"},
		{"id":"group-fastwr","type":"group","x":2240,"y":40,"width":540,"height":1040,"label":"Fast Wrapper Principles"},
		{"id":"legend","type":"text","text":"## Legend\n🔴 **Red** = To Do (ready)\n⬜ **Gray** = Blocked\n🟠 **Orange** = Doing\n🔵 **Cyan** = Ready to review\n🟢 **Green** = Done\n🟣 **Purple** = Proposed by agent","x":-540,"y":-310,"width":380,"height":230,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"canvas-warnings","type":"text","text":"## Warnings\n✓ None","x":-540,"y":-530,"width":380,"height":200,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"canvas-errors","type":"text","text":"## Errors\n✓ None","x":-540,"y":-750,"width":380,"height":200,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"2f8e36c0","type":"text","text":"## FWP-01 Set fast-wrapper rules and success criteria\nDefined the fast-wrapper contract in docs/fast_wrapper_principles.md. The key rules are now explicit: physics formulas and per-step state transitions remain in the existing module files, the wrapper is only allowed to change scheduling/batching/data layout, the current WireEDMEnv.step microstep order is the reference contract, batch_size must be independent of servo_interval, EDMState remains the public API, and the wrapper cannot become the primary performance path until same-seed parity plus the frozen benchmark both pass on the canonical 0.20 mm and 0.05 mm meshes. This card gives FWP-02 a concrete boundary: shared kernels in the modules, thin adapters and scheduler outside them, no second simulator.","x":2300,"y":80,"width":420,"height":480,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"2bd3d2b6","type":"text","text":"## FWP-02 Define shared module kernel boundaries\nMapped the shared kernel boundaries in docs/fast_wrapper_kernel_boundaries.md against the current WireEDMEnv.step order. The result is explicit: ignition and wire already own reusable compiled hot logic, dielectric and mechanics have clear natural step boundaries, and material must keep crater sampling/position increment inside the module while receiving explicit RNG input from the wrapper. The environment continues to own action cadence, time bookkeeping, top-level termination, and control-step obs/reward. This freezes the rule for the next build task: the wrapper may batch and adapt state, but it must call module-owned microstep logic rather than duplicating physics in a second codepath.","x":2300,"y":760,"width":420,"height":280,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"8bfbf72b","type":"text","text":"## FWB-01 Build hot-path state adapters\nCreate a compact hot-path state representation for the wrapper without replacing EDMState as the public API. Add load/store adapters between EDMState and the hot-path buffers, using sentinel values where needed for optional fields, so the wrapper can execute quickly while the rest of the codebase still sees the normal state object.","x":2300,"y":1640,"width":420,"height":280,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"2274fbe2","type":"text","text":"## FWB-02 Make batched randomness reproducible\nDefine how the wrapper gets all random draws for a batch without calling Python inside the hot loop. Pre-generate random pools from env.np_random, define a stable cursor/layout for ignition and crater draws, and ensure the modular runner and wrapper can be compared with the same seed.","x":2300,"y":2420,"width":420,"height":280,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"bdb3c94e","type":"text","text":"## FWB-03 Build the batched wrapper shell\nAdd an optional execution shell that runs many microsteps per Python call while reusing the shared module kernels. The shell should own batch-size handling, early exit, action cadence, and time advancement, but should not introduce separate physics formulas.","x":2300,"y":3200,"width":420,"height":260,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"b930e913","type":"text","text":"## FWB-04 Route all microstep physics through the wrapper\nConnect the wrapper to the real per-microstep physics in ignition, wire, dielectric, material, and mechanics using the shared kernel boundaries. The wrapper must preserve the same update order and feedback path as the modular runner, so it becomes a faster scheduler for the same simulation rather than a second simulator.","x":2300,"y":3980,"width":420,"height":280,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"e2bd6523","type":"text","text":"## FWV-01 Prove wrapper and modular runner match\nAdd same-seed comparison tests that run the modular runner and the fast wrapper against the same workload and assert parity on termination, crater counts, wire temperatures, wire damage, positions, and dashboard-required signals. The wrapper does not graduate until this passes.","x":2300,"y":4760,"width":420,"height":260,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"befe2c34","type":"text","text":"## FWV-02 Benchmark the wrapper and decide promotion\nRun the frozen benchmark and dashboard workflow against the fast wrapper, compare it to the modular baseline, and decide whether the wrapper is good enough to become the primary performance path while the modular runner remains available for debugging and development.","x":2300,"y":5540,"width":420,"height":280,"color":"6"}
	],
